# App permissions — what an app may do on this node

`src/app_perms.py` · `src/data_connector.py` · `src/control/modules/apps.py`

An app used to get one of two answers: everything the data connector offers — its
own section of the mesh, a drawer, a DHT namespace, names, the node's signature on
an assertion — for holding the connector's token, and two grants beside it
(`logs`, `links`) that an operator ticked on the Apps page. That was enough while
the apps were the two that ship with the node. It stops being enough the moment an
app can **drive** the node — call the operations the console calls, or replace one
of them — because "it holds the token" is then the whole answer to "may it restart
this machine?".

So permissions are **named**, arranged in a **tree**, **asked for** in a manifest
and **granted by a person**.

## The tree

Every permission there is, and its level. Nothing outside this list exists: a
manifest that asks for something else is told which name it got wrong.

| Permission | Level | What it allows |
|---|---|---|
| `network` | normal | end-to-end messages on the app's own section |
| `storage` | normal | the app's own encrypted drawer |
| `dht` | normal | entries under the app's own DHT namespace |
| `names` | normal | reading node names, finding a node by name |
| `identity` | normal | assertions signed in the node's name, scoped to this app ([`../AppAuth/guide`](../AppAuth/guide)) |
| `log` | normal | lines in the node's log, attributed to the app |
| `notify` | normal | a problem on the notice board, filed under the app |
| `report` | normal | reporting a node that abuses it (never answered) |
| `readstate` | sensitive | **everything below** — what the console shows, without changing any of it |
| `readstate.node` | sensitive | version, uptime, addresses, links, known nodes, names |
| `readstate.links` | sensitive | the links alone — a medium, a latency, an age (was the `links` grant) |
| `readstate.logs` | sensitive | the whole log, the core's and every app's (was the `logs` grant) |
| `readstate.alerts` | sensitive | the notice board |
| `readstate.trace` | sensitive | the protocol trace |
| `readstate.config` | sensitive | settings and transport options |
| `readstate.trust` | sensitive | trust anchors, witnesses, membership, signing keys held (never the keys) |
| `readstate.updates` | sensitive | releases, packages, the installed set, transfers |
| `readstate.apps` | sensitive | apps, their state, and their read-only operations |
| `control` | dangerous | **everything below** — what an operator at the console can do |
| `control.node` | dangerous | ping, speed test, forget, retry, restart, rename |
| `control.network` | dangerous | probing, punching, listening, discovery, multi-link |
| `control.config` | dangerous | settings and transports |
| `control.diagnostics` | dangerous | starting the trace, keeping the log, clearing alerts |
| `control.trust` | dangerous | trust, revocation, witnesses, invitations, joining a network |
| `control.updates` | dangerous | releases, packages, the store, signing keys, transfers |
| `control.apps` | dangerous | enabling apps and calling the operations they declare — **never** their permissions |
| `control.appweb` | dangerous | the routes an app's own page calls, for an app that declares no operations |
| `modding` | dangerous | standing in front of the node's own operations — before, after, or instead of them |

**A parent covers its children.** Granting `readstate` grants all nine reads;
granting `readstate.logs` grants that one. Taking a parent back takes its children
with it: a page that unticks `readstate` and leaves `readstate.logs` ticked has
said two contradictory things, and the narrower one is kept.

**Three levels, by blast radius.**

- *normal* — what every connector client always had. Allowed when the manifest asks
  for it, and for an app with **no manifest at all** (every app written before
  manifests existed — an upgrade must not take its section away). An operator can
  still take one back.
- *sensitive* — reading this node's state. Off until a person grants it.
- *dangerous* — driving the node or replacing what it does. Off until granted, and
  the page says what it means before the switch moves.

## The manifest

An app says what it wants, and why. At install time from its package — a
`nmesh.json` at the root of its files — or at run time over the connector (the
`MANIFEST` frame, `ConnectorClient.declare`):

```json
{
  "name": "statusboard",
  "title": "Status board",
  "version": "1.2.0",
  "description": "A wall display of this node and its links.",
  "permissions": [
    {"name": "readstate.node", "why": "to show this node's state"},
    {"name": "readstate.links", "why": "to draw its links"},
    "network"
  ],
  "api": [
    {"name": "refresh", "summary": "Redraw now", "changes": true}
  ]
}
```

- **Declaring asks; it grants nothing above normal.** The answer (`PERMS`) says
  what was asked and what is held — the two differ until a person decides.
- **Least privilege is what an app asks for now.** A new manifest that no longer
  asks for something drops the grant; asking for it again does not bring it back.
- **A manifest is hostile input**: at most 32 kB, 32 permissions, 32 operations; a
  name is `[a-z][a-z0-9_]{0,31}`; text is cut to its bound and stripped of anything
  unprintable (a direction override in a "why" is how a reason lies about itself on
  the page that shows it). An unknown *field* is ignored — a newer app on an older
  node — and an unknown *permission* is refused by name.
- **A built-in's manifest is this build's.** Chat, fleet and MCP declare theirs from
  the source at every start, and a client claiming one of their ids cannot rewrite
  what they ask for.
- `api` is optional: the operations the app answers, declared exactly like a
  built-in's ([`../AppAPI/guide`](../AppAPI/guide)). Only an app connected with
  its own token may expose any, and never under a built-in's name.

## Who an app is: its own token

A grant is held by an **app id**, so it is only as good as the proof that the
client asking *is* that app. The connector's shared token cannot be that proof —
every local process that ever held it could claim any id. So each app has **its
own token**, derived from a secret the node keeps (`<data>/connector.secret`, mode
0600, never sent anywhere):

```
token_for(app_id) = "app-" ‖ base64url(HMAC-SHA256(secret, "nmesh-app-token\0" ‖ app_id)[:24])
```

| A client authenticated with… | is answered… |
|---|---|
| the app's own token | everything that app was granted |
| the shared token | the normal set and **nothing above it**, whatever was granted to the id it claims |

The built-in apps connect with their own tokens. An app outside the node gets its
token from the Apps page — *Permissions → Connector token*, on this machine only
(`apps.token` never travels) — or is launched with it
([`../ProcessLauncher/guide`](../ProcessLauncher/guide)). The secret is kept, so a
container given its token once keeps working across restarts.

## What each frame needs

Checked on every frame, against the book as it is *now*: taking a permission back
takes effect on the next frame, not the next connection.

| Frames | Needs |
|---|---|
| `SEND`, and delivery of `RECV` | `network` |
| `STORE_*` | `storage` |
| `APP_DHT_*` | `dht` |
| `PSEUDO_*` | `names` |
| `AUTH_ASSERT`, `AUTH_VERIFY` | `identity` |
| `LOG_WRITE` | `log` |
| `NOTIFY` | `notify` |
| `ABUSE` | `report` |
| `LOG_QUERY`, `LOG_SINCE`, `LOG_WATCH` | `readstate.logs` |
| `LINKS` | `readstate.links` |
| `CONTROL` | the app's own token, then what the operation needs (below) |
| `MOD_HOOK` | the app's own token and `modding` |

A refused question is still **answered** — with the empty answer (`absent`, `[]`,
`refused: true`) — so an app that is not permitted never looks like a node that has
wedged.

## The internal API

`CONTROL` carries one control-plane frame — the same document a console page sends
to `POST /api/control` — and is answered with the plane's reply, matched by the
request's `id`. The app reaches **every operation the console has**, through the
same validation, under an origin of its own (`app:<id>`), and the plane asks the
permission book what that origin may call:

| An operation… | needs |
|---|---|
| that reads (`changes` not declared) | `readstate.<scope>` |
| that changes something | `control.<scope>` |
| `control.catalogue`, `control.changes`, `jobs.poll/list/forget` | nothing more (the catalogue is filtered, a job is visible to the app that started it) |
| `jobs.start` | what the operation it starts needs |
| `apps.call` | `readstate.apps` for a read, `control.apps` for one that changes state — and never an operation declared `operator` |
| `apps.grant`, `apps.permit`, `apps.forget`, `apps.token` | **nothing an app can hold** |

The scope is the module's (`app_perms.MODULE_SCOPE`): `node` and `pseudo` are
`node`; `config` and `transports` are `config`; `trace`, `logs`, `alerts` read as
themselves and write as `diagnostics`; `trust` and `join` are `trust`; `keys`,
`releases`, `packages`, `store`, `transfer` are `updates`; `web` reads as `apps`
and writes as `appweb`. A module that is not mapped is reachable by no app, and
`tests/test_app_perms.py` fails the day the plane grows one.

**Permissions never move themselves.** The four operations that decide what an app
may do are refused to every app origin, whatever it holds. An app with
`control.apps` can enable another app; it cannot give itself, or anybody, anything.

**Internal only, by default.** The connector listens on the loopback (or a Unix
socket, 0600). Nothing here is reachable from the network unless an app puts it
there — which is exactly what the MCP app does, deliberately and behind its own
token ([`../Apps/mcp`](../Apps/mcp)). The **Apps → Internal API** page lists every
endpoint with the permission an app needs for it, the same list the MCP app's tools
come from.

```python
client = ConnectorClient(host, port, token_for_this_app, APP_ID)
await client.connect()
await client.declare({"name": "statusboard", "permissions": ["readstate"]})
reply = await client.control("node.state")      # {"ok": true, "result": {...}}
reply = await client.control("node.restart")    # {"ok": false, "code": "refused",
                                                #  "error": "node.restart needs the control.node permission"}
```

## Apps that declare nothing: their page's routes

Chat and fleet declare a handful of operations; most of what they do lives behind
the routes their own pages call. Writing each again as an operation would be a
second implementation of every one, so the routes are **read from what the browser
is sent** (`src/app_web.py`): the bundle the console serves for the page is scanned
for the calls it makes — `api("/api/chat/send", "POST", …)` — with the method each
uses. What the page can do is, by construction, what is offered:

- `web.routes` lists them, per app with a page that is running;
- `web.request` calls one, exactly as listed (method and path), replayed over the
  loopback against this console under a session that exists for that one call.

`web.request` **never leaves this machine** — a managed node is not a jump host,
and this would make it one — and an app reaches it only with `control.appweb`.

## Modding

An app holding `modding` may stand in front of one of the node's operations:

```python
async def tag(payload):            # {"op", "mode", "params", "result"?}
    return {"result": dict(payload["result"], shown_by="statusboard")}
await client.hook("node.state", "after", tag)
```

| Mode | The hook is asked | and answers |
|---|---|---|
| `before` | with the arguments | `{"params": …}` — new arguments |
| `replace` | with the arguments | `{"result": …}` — the answer, instead of the node's |
| `after` | with the arguments and the node's answer | `{"result": …}` — a changed answer |

What makes that something a node **survives** rather than something it is at the
mercy of:

- **The native operation is the fallback.** A hook that raises, answers garbage, or
  takes longer than `_MOD_TIMEOUT` (5 s) is ignored — the call is answered as if
  there were none, and the failure is written down where an operator looks.
- **What a mod hands on is checked again.** Arguments from a `before` hook are bound
  against the declaration like a caller's: an undeclared one or a wrong kind falls
  back to the caller's own.
- **A mod never sees its own calls**, or a hook that calls what it hooks would
  recurse.
- **One hook per operation**, and it belongs to the client that installed it: it
  goes when the client does, and it is asked of the book at every call — taking
  `modding` back takes every hook with it at once.
- **What says what the node is cannot be modded**: `apps.*`, `control.*`, `jobs.*`
  and `web.*`. A mod that could rewrite the catalogue or a grant could hide itself
  from the page that exists to show it. The Internal API page marks every modded
  operation with the app that mods it.

## In the console

*Apps → Installed*: each app's card says how many permissions it asked for and how
many it holds; **Permissions** opens the list — only what it asked for, each with
its level, the node's description and the app's own reason. A dangerous one asks
for confirmation first. *Connected apps* lists every app that is not built in and
declared a manifest: attached now (with its own token or only the shared one),
installed with a `nmesh.json`, or remembered from before; it is where an app's
connector token is read (this machine only) and where an app is forgotten.

*Apps → Internal API*: every endpoint, what it does, the permission an app needs,
and the MCP tool it becomes.

## Control-plane operations

| Operation | Reach | What it does |
|---|---|---|
| `apps.permissions` | remote | every app's manifest, what it asked for and holds, and the tree |
| `apps.permit` | govern | give or take back one permission from one app |
| `apps.forget` | govern | drop an app that is not built in, and its grants |
| `apps.token` | this machine only | the connector token one app authenticates with |
| `apps.api` | remote | every endpoint, with the permission it needs and its MCP tool |
| `web.routes` | remote | the routes each app's page calls |
| `web.request` | this machine only | call one of them |

Granting is `govern`, not `manage`: what an app may do on a node is a decision
about trust, like pinning a signing key, and an operator handed a node's console to
restart a service was not handed that.

## Storage

`<data>/app_perms.json`, mode 0600, written atomically: per app id, the manifest and
the grants a person made. Bounded (`MAX_APPS` = 64; the app with no grant seen
longest ago goes first, a built-in never). Read defensively: a corrupt file, a grant
that is not the literal `true`, an id that is not 16 lowercase hex characters, a
permission that does not exist — each is nothing granted, never a crash. The two
grants of `apps.json` (`logs`, `links`) are carried over once, as they were, and
never again after an operator changes them.
